KNOWNBUG
main.cpp

^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ manipulator for os<char>: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ manipulator for os<wchar_t>: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
The operator call is resolved with the function template name as an
untyped argument.  deduce_without_call_arguments (cpp_typecheck_resolve.cpp)
then deduces endl's arguments from the first complete specialization of os
in the symbol table, so one of the two calls gets endl<the other type>,
which only converts to bool: operator<<(bool) is chosen.  Which one depends
on the symbol table's order (with libc++, `std::cerr << std::endl' in a file
named main.cpp, cpp11_iostream_cerr_libcxx).  Each candidate's parameter
type has to drive the deduction instead ([temp.deduct.funcaddr]/1).
